; ============================================================
; TITLE:    The Wandering Turtles
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     random, several turtles, distance, square root
; SUMMARY:  Four turtles take random steps on a grid, and the program checks how far a random walker really gets.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Four turtles start in the middle. Each one, again and again, picks
;   one of the four directions at random and takes a step of 10. Their
;   colored trails spread out like ink in water. At the end the program
;   prints how far each turtle ended up from home and compares it with
;   the square-root rule for random walks.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and watch. Run again for a different walk. Change the
;   number of steps in the last line.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * RANDOM to make choices
;   * ASK to move several turtles
;   * DISTANCE to measure how far a turtle is from a point
;   * a surprising fact: after N steps you are usually about the
;     square root of N steps from where you started
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Allow 8 directions (turn by multiples of 45).
;   * Make the turtles walk 4 times as long. Do they get 4 times as far?
;   * Use WRAP so turtles that leave on one side come back on the other.
; ============================================================

; The random walk is a classic turtle experiment: it shows how chance,
; repeated, has a shape of its own. A walker who takes N steps of
; length L, each in a random direction, ends up on average roughly
; L times the square root of N from the start (not N times L, as you
; might first guess, because many steps cancel out).

make "colors [[230 60 60] [40 140 230] [40 170 80] [240 160 20]]

to setup
  clearscreen
  setbackground [248 246 240]
  grid
  foreach [0 1 2 3] [
    setturtle ?
    penup home pendown
    setpensize 2
    setpencolor item ? + 1 :colors
    setshape "circle
    showturtle
  ]
end

; A pale grid, so you can see the walkers stay on the lines.
to grid
  setpencolor [225 222 210]
  setpensize 1
  for [x -400 400 10] [penup setxy :x -300 pendown setxy :x 300]
  for [y -300 300 10] [penup setxy -400 :y pendown setxy 400 :y]
end

; One random step: face one of the four directions and walk 10.
to wander
  setheading 90 * random 4
  forward 10
end

to walk :steps
  repeat :steps [
    foreach [0 1 2 3] [ask ? [wander]]
    if (remainder repcount 5) = 0 [wait 1]
  ]
end

to report :steps
  print (sentence [After] :steps [steps of 10:])
  localmake "total 0
  foreach [0 1 2 3] [
    localmake "d ask.distance ?
    make "total :total + :d
    print (sentence [turtle] ? [is] round :d [steps from home])
  ]
  print (sentence [Their average is] round :total / 4 [steps.])
  print (sentence [The square-root rule says about] round 10 * sqrt :steps [steps.])
  print [With only four walkers the average can be far off. Run it again!]
end

to ask.distance :who
  setturtle :who
  output distance [0 0]
end

setup
walk 600
report 600
